# Workflow Recipe

This workflow was used to generate the bioinformatic results for the paper: ["Transfer potential of F-like plasmids in *Escherichia coli* differs by animal environment"](https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.02.02.703319) by Sneha Sundar, Sebastian Bonhoeffer and Jana S. Huisman. 

It has been specifically designed for:
* isolate bacterial genomes 
* for running on HPC systems that use `Slurm` as the job scheduler. 

Given a bacterial isolate genome with extension `*.fna` or `*.fna.gz`, this workflow can do the following: 
* decompress genome 
* annotate bacterial/plasmid using [Bakta](https://github.com/oschwengers/bakta) (v. 1.8.1,)
* run [tBLASTn](https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-421) (BLAST+ 2.12.0) on genomes using a pre-defined query gene set. 
* identify phage or plasmid associated contigs in genome using [geNOMAD](https://github.com/apcamargo/genomad) (v. 1.7.4) 

* assess genome quality using [checkM2](https://github.com/chklovski/CheckM2) (v.1.1.0)
* identify plasmid replicons using [PlasmidFinder](https://github.com/genomicepidemiology/plasmidfinder) (v. 2.1.6)
* reconstruct plasmids using MOB-recon from [MOB-Suite](https://github.com/phac-nml/mob-suite) (v.3.1.9)
* compute pairwise average nucleotide identity using [fastANI](https://github.com/ParBLiSS/FastANI) (v.1.34)
* output contig lengths for all contigs in a genome with (custom script)
* specifically filter plasmid or virus associated contigs using geNOMAD classification score threshold (custom script)
  

## Set-up

### Conda/mamba Environment requirements:
All required conda/mamba environments need to be installed in a specific directory, say `miniconda3/envs`. This directory path needs to be specified in `config/config.yaml` under `conda_env`. 

Specifications to install each environment are in `code/find-genes-workflow/envs`. 

### HPC system environment module requirements
To run tblastn using a reference set of *tra* genes as queries against our genomes, we used a module available in our HPC system's software stack `blast-plus/2.12.0`. This specific module name would need to be modified depending on the user's specific HPC system. 

Alternatively, blast can also be installed as a conda envvironment. The associated rule would have to be modified if this is the case. See [snakemake doc](https://snakemake.readthedocs.io/en/stable/snakefiles/deployment.html#using-already-existing-conda-environments) for more info on this. 

### Database requirements
[Bakta](https://github.com/oschwengers/bakta), [geNOMAD](https://github.com/apcamargo/genomad), [PlasmidFinder](https://github.com/genomicepidemiology/plasmidfinder), and [MOB-Suite](https://github.com/phac-nml/mob-suite) require an external database. Download them as needed in a separate directory, say `code/find-genes-workflow/databases`.


### Input genomes:
All genomes should be in one directory. All should have an extension `*.fna` or `*.fna.gz`. `*` should be the genome id and should be unique to each genome. The path to this genome directory should be specified in `config/config.yaml` under `genomes_folder`.

A file containing all the input genome ids, with each genome id on a separate line, is required. This tells snakemake which genomes in the directory should be analysed. Two examples of this file  `config/BioSample_ecoli.txt` and `config/test.txt`. 
The path to this file should be specified in `config/config.yaml` under `samples`. 

### Config 
Modify the configuration file in `config/config.yaml` to reflect your analysis. 
Input paths and output paths along with parameters and database paths to specific rules need to be specified. The specific parameters currently provided are the ones used in this paper. 

### Profile
`profile/config.yaml` specifies parameters for a specific snakemake run. For more details see this [snakemake documentation](https://snakemake.readthedocs.io/en/stable/executing/cli.html#executing-profiles). This needs to be modified to suit your specific computing environment. 


Rule-specific resources specified here reflect typical requirements to run on a single *E.coli* genome. Modify as needed. 

### Specifying target rules

Target rules specify which output files you want so that snakemake can determine which rules to run to obtain the specific output files. 

Let's say you want to run bakta on your genomes. You would then:
1. go to `Snakefile`
2. Write `get_bakta_output()` under `rule all: input:`

All the functions that list the output files based on your input genomes are provided in `common.smk`.

You could also specific output files from multiple rules: `get_bakta_output(),get_tblasnt_output()`


## Running the workflow 

Once the set-up is finished . All that remains is to run the workflow:

1. Activate your snakemake environment. 
2. Run the bash script in `run_snakemake.sh`
   
If you would like to perform a dry-run add the `-n` flag to the command in `run_snakemake.sh.` 
